<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Celebration · 1765 · Central &amp; East Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Mo Li Hua</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="zh">茉莉花</bdi> / <bdi lang="zh-Latn">Mòlìhuā</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Chinese folk song about the jasmine flower, sung for more than 200 years. Puccini used its tune in his opera Turandot.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Han Chinese of the Jiangnan region</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chinese</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Jiangnan region (around Suzhou, Shanghai and Hangzhou), China</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>usually dated to the Qianlong reign (1735–1796); earliest lyrics in the Ming Wanli period; transcribed 1793, printed in Europe 1804</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Celebration</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua</a>&quot;, &quot;Jasmine Flower&quot;, is a Chinese folk song of the xiaodiao or &quot;short tune&quot; genre, popular in the towns of the Jiangnan region south of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>. It is usually dated to the reign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> (1735–1796) of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, though the ethnomusicologist Frederick Lau notes that the earliest &quot;Molihua&quot; lyrics appeared in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>'s Wanli period. It uses the five-note pentatonic scale, and its many regional versions differ in words and melody: one describes the custom of giving jasmine flowers, popular in the southern Yangtze delta, another the fear of plucking the flower. It is also known as &quot;Xian Hua Diao&quot;, &quot;Fresh Flower Melody&quot;, and in the north as &quot;La Mei Hua&quot; or &quot;Yu Mei Hua&quot;.</p><p>John Barrow, a member of George Macartney's embassy to the Qianlong court, was the first to write the tune in Western notation, in 1793. When he published it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1804 he noted that it seemed to be one of the most popular songs in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and his publication gave it, in Lau's words, a &quot;grand entrance on the world stage&quot;. It became one of the first Chinese folk songs widely known outside China. From 1896 Qing officials in Europe sometimes used it as a temporary national anthem, until &quot;Cup of Solid Gold&quot; became the Qing state's official anthem in 1911. At the start of the 20th century travelling musicians played it across China, and the music historian August Wilhelm Ambros analysed it in his unfinished History of Music.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giacomo-puccini">Giacomo Puccini</a>, who began his last opera Turandot in 1920, heard the tune on a music box brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by an Italian diplomat, probably Baron Edoardo Fassini-Camoss; its sheets misnamed the song &quot;Sinfa&quot;. In Turandot (1926), set in Imperial China, it became the leitmotif of the princess's splendour, first heard in the boys' chorus &quot;Là, sui monti dell'est&quot;. Puccini's version gained a life of its own: Tan Dun drew on it in his Symphony 1997, written for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handover-of-hong-kong">handover of Hong Kong</a>. In Hollywood it appeared in The Good Earth (1937), and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> some films used it to stand for the Chinese.</p><p>In 1982 the song was placed on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> list of recommended songs. Said to be a favourite of the Communist Party general secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiang-zemin">Jiang Zemin</a>, it was played at his request at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handover-of-hong-kong">handover of Hong Kong</a> in 1997, and at that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macau">Macau</a> in 1999; it was heard at Party Central Committee meetings and as hold music in provincial government offices. A Chinese girl and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> students sang it at the closing ceremony of the 2004 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> Olympics to introduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>, and an adaptation by Tan Dun and Wang Hesheng, chosen from more than 4,000 pieces, opened the victory theme at the medal ceremonies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-summer-olympics">Beijing 2008</a> Games. It was also played at the ceremony awarding the 2010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> to Liu Xiaobo.</p><p>During the 2011 Chinese pro-democracy protests, called the Jasmine Revolution, protesters reposted videos of Party officials singing the song and suggested playing it on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mobile-phone">mobile phones</a> as a deniable, hard-to-block sign of support for democracy. The authorities put the song on their list of censored material: videos, including one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-jintao">Hu Jintao</a> singing it in Kenya in 2006, were removed, searches for its name were blocked, and jasmine flowers themselves were restricted. Activists wrote a new version of their own. The song is still widely performed, by Celine Dion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> Zuying at the 2013 CCTV New Year's Gala and by Dimash Qudaibergen since 2018, and has been called a national musical icon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arirang">Arirang</a>&quot; and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakura-sakura">Sakura Sakura</a>&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song in praise of the jasmine flower, its beauty and scent. Its regional versions turn this in different ways: one around the custom of giving jasmine as a gift, another around the wish to pick a flower and the fear of doing so.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Chinese</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Chinese</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="zh">好一朵茉莉花，好一朵茉莉花，满园的花开赛不过了它，本待要，采一朵戴，又恐怕看花的骂；本待要，采一朵戴，又恐怕看花的骂。</span><span class="sg-r" lang="zh-Latn">Hǎo yī duǒ mòlìhuā, hǎo yī duǒ mòlìhuā, mǎn yuán de huā kāi sài bù guò le tā, běn dài yào, cǎi yī duǒ dài, yòu kǒngpà kān huā de mà; běn dài yào, cǎi yī duǒ dài, yòu kǒngpà kān huā de mà.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>What a fine jasmine flower, what a fine jasmine flower; of all the flowers blooming in the garden, none can outdo it. I meant to pick one to wear, but I fear the one who tends the flowers would scold; I meant to pick one to wear, but I fear the one who tends the flowers would scold.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional (Jiangsu). Text from <a href="https://zh.wikisource.org/wiki/%E8%8C%89%E8%8E%89%E8%8A%B1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (Hanyu Pinyin (tone marks)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>The edition prints the whole song as one line, and so do the English and the romanization. It is in simplified characters.</li><li>'好一朵' (hǎo yī duǒ): an exclamation, 'what a fine (single) flower'; 朵 is the measure word for flowers.</li><li>'赛不过了它' (sài bù guò le tā): 'cannot outdo it, cannot surpass it'.</li><li>'本待要' (běn dài yào): 'I had meant to, I was about to'.</li><li>'看花的' (kān huā de): 'the one who watches over the flowers', the garden's keeper; 看 read kān ('guard'). Singers often say kàn ('look at'), which would make it 'those who come to look at the flowers'.</li><li>rom: base tones written (一 yī, 不 bù), not the sandhi forms yí/yì and bú that are sung.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="YkfAzMlL3xM" data-title="Mo Li Hua: Zhang Ye, the Jiangsu folk song (CCTV)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Zhang Ye, the Jiangsu folk song (CCTV)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel 中国舞台. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YkfAzMlL3xM" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Turandot, opera by Giacomo Puccini</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1926</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (leitmotif of the princess)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Victory theme of the Beijing 2008 Olympic medal ceremonies (Tan Dun and Wang Hesheng)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony 1997 (Tan Dun)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1997</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in, after Puccini's setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Étude sur un thème chinois, Op. 25, No. 3 (Anton Arensky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1890s</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Good Earth, film</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1937</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Nine Songs: Water Spirit (Tan Dun)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1990</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in, after Puccini's setting</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Protest version by Chinese activists</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2011</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Recording by Kenny G</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Duet by Celine Dion and Song Zuying, CCTV New Year's Gala</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2013</span><span class="sg-chain-h">cover version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Avatar: The Last Airbender, television series</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2005–2008</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, as a musical theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Civilization VI, video game</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2016</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, as China's theme</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Zhu-Zhu, music video (Glukoza and Leningrad)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2018</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li></ul></section>
